# ─── Key material and secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This repository is PUBLIC and a slop machine node deploys behind the
# connector, so it generates the same key material every other TOON box does:
# an ILP signer, the settlement keys that actually hold value, and peering
# secrets. These rules exist before any of that is generated, on purpose — the
# failure mode they prevent (connector#490) is an operator running `git add -A`
# in a checkout on a live box and committing funded private keys to a public
# repo.
#
# Wildcarded rather than listed by name so a re-derived, per-box, or
# additional key cannot reintroduce the gap by being named slightly
# differently. An ignore rule does not protect a file that is ALREADY tracked
# — if one ever lands, `git rm --cached` it and rotate the key; the rotation
# is what closes the exposure, not the rule.
*.key
*.pem
*.secret

# The operator surface's credentials, and on a HUB box all three sit in one
# directory (deploy/hub/), which is why the third is named the way it is:
#
#   operator-bearer.token   a secret outright. It gates the connector's reads.
#   operator-write.keys     the ALLOWLIST — public halves, one keyid per line.
#     (plural)              Not secret, but per box, and ADR 0008 revokes
#                           authority by editing it ON the box; a committed
#                           allowlist puts revocation behind a pull request
#                           and re-creates two sources of truth.
#   operator-signing.key    the slot app's PRIVATE ed25519 seed — the
#     (singular)            credential that mutates a hub's routing table.
#                           Called `operator-write.key` it would sit one
#                           character from the allowlist above: two files a
#                           tab-completion apart, one the secret that holds
#                           the authority and the other the file you hand-edit
#                           to take it away. It is caught by `*.key` too; it
#                           is listed here by NAME because a wildcard is a
#                           rule you have to know and a name is one you can
#                           read.
operator-bearer.token
operator-write.keys
operator-signing.key
*.allow

# Wallets and chain deploy output — private keys or per-cluster state.
*-wallet.json
*-keypair.json
testnet-wallets.json

# ─── Environment files ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every `.env*` variant, not the bare name: operator copies like
# `.env.bak-20260718` slipped past a bare `.env` rule and got committed
# (connector, store). Checked-in templates are re-allowed explicitly.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
!.env.template

# ─── Operator snapshot copies ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Point-in-time scratch from live-box edits, never configuration the repo
# should carry. `*.bak[0-9]*` covers `.bak2-`, `.bak3-` — a second snapshot of
# the same file in one session, which matched none of the other rules
# (connector#1004).
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.bak.*
*.bak[0-9]*
*.orig
*.rej
*~

# ─── Media and stream output ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Generated segments, playlists, and recordings — bulky, reproducible, and
# never source.
#
# NOTE: these are matched by DIRECTORY, not by extension, and deliberately so.
# HLS segments are MPEG-TS files ending in `.ts`, which is also the TypeScript
# extension — a `*.ts` rule here would silently ignore every source file if
# this repo is written in TypeScript (as relay and toon-client are). Do not
# add one. Keep generated media confined to these paths instead.
#
# The corollary: `segments/` matches a directory of that name ANYWHERE, so no
# source directory may be called `segments`. The origin's segmenter lives in
# `packages/station-origin/src/segmenter/` for exactly this reason — an earlier
# `src/segments/` was silently untracked, and the image build was what noticed.
# Do not weaken the rule to make a source directory visible; rename it.
segments/
recordings/
media-out/
*.m3u8

# ─── Build output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The stack is TypeScript on Node (see CLAUDE.md); the Rust shape is left
# covered because these rules predate that choice and cost nothing to keep.
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/
dist/
target/
*.tsbuildinfo
.turbo/
.eslintcache
coverage/
*.tgz

# ─── Local state ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
data/
.docker-data/
.cache/
.devbox/
/result

# ─── Logs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
logs/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# ─── Editor and OS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ─── Agent tooling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local Claude Code config; skills/ are project deliverables and stay tracked.
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/
**/.claude/worktrees/
.mcp.json
